 part of the u.s. total force we  Rumsfeld said it February of 2006 so we  should be entitled to the same immunity  from civilian litigation as the US  military is and Blackwater twice has has  appealed to the US Supreme Court which I  should point out is dominated by  Republican appointees and and the Chief  Justice is an appointee of the current  president and yet the Supreme Court  rejected Blackwater's appeals so it  appears now as though this case is going  to go forward in state court in North  Carolina where I understand there would  be no cap on potential damages and the  lawyers for those four families are  comparing it to the tobacco litigation  of the 1990s saying that once the first  domino falls it could cause shockwaves  to ride through the industry in fact a  KBR has filed an amicus brief in the  case which way which gives you a sense  of how important it is to that industry  have they specified the amount of  damages they're seeking not yet and the  Marines were just outside Fallujah right  did the Greens come to the aid of these  Blackwater guys that were caught in that  ambush no the Marines and I tell the  story in the book I think the Marines  made a determination that if that that  it was already done the guys were dead  and hanging from the bridge and if they  had gone in in some kind of a  heavy-handed manner I think that they  felt that they would have been  subjecting US soldiers to unnecessary  severe danger and I think that the  Marines made a calculation that they had  to let the scene play out and so it  wasn't an immediate response and the  Marines  and also the 82nd airborne both had been  trying various approaches to dealing  with Fallujah and I think that they were  trying to take what they call the hearts  and minds approach to the city rather  than what ended up happening which was a  crushing of the city if that had been  Marine unit that had been ambushed I can  almost guarantee you the Marines would  have gone in and it's interesting  because I believe the previous morning  Marines had been ambushed had been  ambushed and killed and the Marines did  come in - outside of the city to do a  recovery operation so yes I mean I think  it's interesting that the guy that these  guys were out in the desert on the night  of March 30th these Blackwater guys the  day before the ambush and they got lost  and they actually made contact with a  military base and I quote one source in  the book as saying that when they  arrived at the military base they  wouldn't tell any of the commanders what  their mission was and one of the  commanders on the base said that they  were like Cowboys and I you know so  there could have been a lot of  resentment toward them I don't know if  that personally isn't why they went in I  think they didn't go in because of what  the commander said that it would have  just subjected Marines to unnecessary  violence when the damage had already  been done what did the Blackwater people  know about conditions in Fallujah before  they tried to drive through I think  everyone in Iraq knew that Fallujah was  a no-go zone and was a dangerous place  it had been the scene of intense  fighting already between US forces and  Iraqis that are also been some serious  crackdowns inside the city on Iraqi  demonstrations in fact the the contract  that governed their mission specifically  named Fallujah as a dangerous area of  the country and and talked about the  threat of Fallujah what these individual  men knew about Fallujah I don't know I  would imagine that they had the kind of  knowledge that everyone on the ground  had that Fallujah is a pretty dangerous  place although at least one of those  guys on that mission hadn't even been in  Iraq more than a few days before he  found himself in the middle of what some  people would say was the most dangerous  city in the world do these contractors  have access to US intelligence at some  level I mean the individual guys on the  ground don't but there there is a  privatized Green Zone within the Green  Zone where there's some limited  coordination of the private military  companies amongst each other and so they  share information  they have daily updates on one what's  going on in various parts of the country  but I think that there's a lot of  tension between the official US military  and the contractors there's also  indications that the contractors maybe  even have better intelligence in some  cases and are not bound by the same  kinds of chain of command issues after a  Blackwater helicopter was brought down  in January in Iraq one of the pilots his  brother had been killed in that  operation he also was a Blackwater pilot  wrote an article in which he said that  you know when people are really in  trouble in Iraq they don't call the US  military they call Blackwater because we  can respond faster you mentioned the  downing of the Blackwater helicopter on  are there other incidents in which  Blackwater personnel have been engaged  in direct military combat right there  have been at least three or four  ambushes on Blackwater convoys and then  I tell one story which I find  particularly disturbing it happened on  April 4th 2004 just five days after the  Fallujah ambush so remember you know the  Blackwater guys are at the center of an  increasingly outrage Sunni resistance in  Fallujah after the killing of four of  its men and at the same time Paul Bremer  was really intent on this handover of  sovereignty happening on June 30th of  2004 he wanted to hand over sovereignty  and get out of Iraq and and so the the  Bremer and the administration the Bush  administration made a determination we  have to crush the Sunni resistance but  we also have to deal with moqtada  al-sadr the leader of the the perhaps  the most powerful Shiite resistance  groups the the mehdi army and as as the  Fallujah incident was happening Bremer  orders the arrest of one of muqtada  al-sadr staff deputies and and they  believed that he was being held in Nadja  so al Sadr swallowers march on Nadja  hundreds and hundreds of them and they  descend on the u.s. occupation office in  Nadja of the Coalition Provisional  Authority building and lo and behold  it's being guarded by Blackwater so on  this morning of April 4th 2004 this  massive demonstration takes place  there's about a half a dozen Blackwater  troops on the roof of the CPA building  and by chance there were a handful of  Salvadoran soldiers who were part of the  Coalition of the Willing and active duty  US Marine a guy named corporal Lonnie  young  so this guy corporal Lonnie young finds  himself on the roof with Blackwater guys  and Salvadoran troops and they're  looking down into the crowd there are  varying stories as to how the shooting  happened but it did start had you know  it did happen the Blackwater guys would  say that the they were fired on from the  crowd journalists on the scene say that  concussion grenades had been fired from  the roof where the Blackwater guys were  and that that started the fighting in  any case a gun battle begins  Lonnie young this Marine has his heavy  machine gun pointed into the crowd he  says and I got the official Marine  account from from Lonnie Young's  testimony he says he has a guy in his  sight with an ak-47 he wants to open  fire on him but there's no commanding  officer on the scene and so he says to  the Blackwater mercenaries sir I've  acquired a target with your permission I  want to open fire and the Blackwater guy  gives the go-ahead to an active duty US  Marine to open fire on an Iraqi  protester armed he says and so that day  it was Blackwater that was in charge of  Salvadoran soldiers it was Blackwater  that was in charge of an active duty US  Marine that to me embodies the serious  lines that have been crossed in fact at  one point during that operation  Blackwater called for reinforcements  from Paul Bremer's Blackwater security  detail and they sent their little bird  helicopters to bring in reinforcements  to the rooftop in Nadja for this  day-long firefight I guess you could  argue whether it's a good or a bad thing  to have have these contractors but or  maybe it's just a necessary thing but it  definitely is a phenomenon of 21st  century warfare at least as practiced by  the United States and you say now  Rumsfeld has officially declared as a  matter of policy that these contractors  are integral to US operations do you  think that's a with malice aforethought  or do you think that we just stumbled  into this well I I definitely think that  the companies themselves were engaged in  in a very serious business plan for a  long time I think that war is profitable  and that that in and of itself is a real  problem that monetary gain is a motive  for armed  that and and for Rumsfeld I think that  he himself has spent his career working  on behalf of the cause of privatization  both he and Dick Cheney you know I don't  I don't mean to presume you know to know  what Rumsfeld thinks but I know from  from hit the publicly available  statements and writings of Donald  Rumsfeld that this was a man who  believed in running the Pentagon and its  war machine like a business like a  corporation and I think that this is  very much a product of that you know  that there's a necessary  I think resistance to privatization when  you when you look at government services  and essential services and I think that  this administration has opened the door  wide as wide as I think it's ever been  in history to these private companies  coming in and the real danger is they're  dealing in human life they're dealing in  war they're dealing in national security  issues  these are life-and-death issues and when  you put it into the private sector and  away from public oversight and  transparency it creates a Frankenstein  laboratory type atmosphere which i think  is playing out in a very scary way in  Iraq right now but who's driving this  the companies or the Defense Department  is all of it well I don't know I don't  think it's the Defense Department i I  think that the that the administration  has this administration that has been  more anti military than most  administrations in history they've  subjected they force these commanders on  the ground into totally unwinnable  situations this is a political war this  is an engagement for national security  of the United States this is an  offensive aggressive war and I think it  defeats the the core reason why a lot of  people join the military we hear  testimony after testimony from soldiers  on the ground who don't know why they're  over there and and Blackwater knows why  it's over there  Blackwater is over there to be at the  vanguard of an offensive war protecting  the most senior US officials in Iraq  that to me is a problem and then when  you look at the fact that they're  bankrolling the politicians that press  most passionately for the war then you  see a sort of system that I think raises  a lot of serious questions put aside for  a moment the whole debate over whether  we should have gotten into this war in  the first place just accept that we are  where we are  could we  be fighting this war without Blackwater  and the other military contracting firms  I don't think that the Bush  administration would be able to sustain  the occupation of Iraq without  Blackwater and the other private  military firms and that's the exact  reason why I think there's a serious  problem with it I don't I don't think  that the American people believe in this  war anymore I don't think that the  military believes in this war and so  they the Bush administration needs the  private sector they need companies like  Blackwater and KBR and Triple Canopy and  DynCorp they need these companies to  keep the war going and and and that to  me is one of the most dramatic examples  of how dangerous these developments have  been you no longer rely on your  citizenry to wage wars you rely on  private companies and and that in and of  itself I think is a serious problem you  rattled off some names eroge  describe to me the leaders in this  industry well I mean Halliburton is is  probably the most famous you know war  contractor and a lot of people pick on  Halliburton because of Dick Cheney's  connection to to the company and they  talk about the cronyism Halliburton was  a very successful company when Bill  Clinton was president and Clinton was  was a major supporter of privatization  of the military bureaucracy and so  Halliburton has emerged as the the  largest contractor in the war and its  former subsidiary KVR  these are come this is a company and the  two of them together that have 16  billion dollars plus in contracts for  the Iraq war DynCorp is a company that  is a private military company like  Blackwater that you probably encountered  when you were in Bosnia DynCorp was  heavily involved with the the wars in  the Balkans continues to be a major war  contractor on the ground in Iraq but I  sort of look at DynCorp as like the  Walmart of private security whereas  Blackwater is sort of like the you know  it's it's it's kind of like the Prada I  mean these these are the this is the  these are high-end guys DynCorp provides  a wide variety of soldiers from various  backgrounds Blackwater provides elite  soldiers and so you you have KBR  Halliburton in Blackwater I think become  very interesting because they're so  politically connected to the Bush  administration but in reality those two  companies are two in a sea of a lot of  war contractors that are making a lot of  money  I remember during Abu Ghraib it turned  out that a lot of the people under  investigation were not in fact MPs but  were contractors who were working as  translators for the interrogation teams  right and there there's there are  lawsuits now that have stemmed from that  civilian lawsuits um the the companies  that have been identified as allegedly  being involved at Abu Ghraib Titan khaki  I even though you raised that I still  think many Americans are unaware of the  fact that it wasn't just Lindy England  who is at Abu Ghraib you know and that's  one of the real problems here it's the  the the grunts on the ground are being  prosecuted or court-martialed for  infractions that appear to go very high  up the chain of command and in the case  of contractors they're not being  prosecuted either and so oftentimes it's  just the you know these kids who signed  up to be in the Pennsylvania National  Guard to go and stop floods and find  themselves in the middle of some  hellhole somewhere and they're really  the ones who are the scapegoats for a  much much bigger scandal so we've talked  almost exclusively about the role of  these firms in Iraq where else are they  at work for the u.s. government well one  one last thing I wanted to say you know  to go back to your other point forty  cents of every dollar that's being spent  on the occupation of Iraq is going to  private military to war contractors not  just private military contractors four  billion dollars has been spent on  private security services in Iraq alone  now companies like Blackwater  increasingly have their sights set on  domestic deployments inside of the  United States I was down in New Orleans  after Hurricane Katrina hit I was  standing on a corner on Bourbon Street  in the French Quarter talking to two New  York City police officers I'm from New  York and an unmarked car speeds up next  to us and out of the car pop four just  bulky guys wearing flak jackets khaki  uniforms m4 machine guns another pistol  strapped to their legs and they come up  to the cops and they say you know where  are the rest of the Blackwater guys and  the New York police officers say oh  there's a lot of them they're down the  street and they tell them the hotel  where they are and then they get back  into the car and they speed off no  license plates on the car and I said to  the to the police officers Blackwater  even like the guys in Iraq and he said  oh yeah they're all over the place here  so I found some of these Blackwater guys  on the street and just struck up a  conversation with them and I said and so  what are you guys doing down here what's  your mission and they said we  here to confront criminals and stop  looters and I said under whose authority  are you down here and kind of fumbled  with each other and then one guy pulls a  gold law enforcement badge he says I was  deputized by the governor of the state  of Louisiana and I was saying you know  you guys on federal contract down here  you're in a state contract and they said  well we can't really talk about it but  we're sleeping in a camp organized by  the Department of Homeland Security so I  looked into it a little bit further and  I was able to determine that in fact the  Department of Homeland Security had  hired Blackwater to engage in security  operations in New Orleans after  Hurricane Katrina and when I got the  contracts it turns out that Blackwater  guys in the ground had told me they were  getting paid three hundred and fifty  dollars a day Blackwater was billing the  federal government nine hundred and  fifty dollars per day per man four  Blackwater operators deployed in the  hurricane zone at one point David they  had six hundred men stretched from Texas  through Mississippi and the Gulf they  were pulling in about two hundred and  forty thousand dollars a day for  security operations in the hurricane  zone at a time when there was a scandal  over two thousand dollar debit cards for  people who were internally displaced  that figure you used four billion  dollars on security in Iraq right is  that four billion dollars spent by the  US government yes that's uh that's  according to representative Henry Waxman  that's something that came out of the  initial hearing he held on Blackwater On  February 7th in the Oversight Committee  so these are taxpayer dollars oh yeah  absolutely so how does the taxpayer know  that he's getting his money's worth put  aside whether it's a good or a bad thing  how do you know you're getting your  money's worth you don't and that's and  that's a major controversy in Congress  right now representative jan Schakowsky  who's on the the Select Intelligence  Committee since 2004 when I've been  talking to her about this issue of  contractors she's been trying to get  information just tell me how many  contractors there are in Iraq tell me  how many contracts we have in excess of  five million dollars tell me how many  have been killed tell me now how many  have been wounded tell me how many  investigations are open at the Justice  Department no answers to any of these  questions and so for years you have  several Congress people some of them in  influential positions who have been  unable to get any information about the  operations of these companies that's  starting to change but it took Henry wax  and since the Fallujah ambush happened  of those Blackwater guys he's been  investigating this one contract he  started aggressively doing it in  November of 2004  it took him until February 7th 2007 to  just get an answer as to whose contract  that ultimately was and it turns out it  was a COS subcontract with KBR and so  now KBR is being forced to give back to  the federal government 20 million  dollars for unauthorized use of security  services in Iraq and they inform their  shareholders that they may have to  return as much as 400 million dollars if  the army continues its investigation I  think earlier you used a figure 100,000  right security consultants know there's  100,000 private contractors that drives  all the KBR right and then of the  military contractors maybe a fifth of  that 100,000 are engaged in military  type activities so that would include  for instance CBS hires a security firm  in Iraq to protect its staff that they  would be included in the hundred  thousand but not in the twenty thousand  right the well I ac